    "validation_file": {
      "id": "1",
      "name": "test.py",
      "contents": "import ast\nfrom unittest import TestCase\nfrom unittest.mock import patch\nfrom unittest_runner import ValidationProtocol\n\n\ndef _calls_named(node, name):\n    \"\"\"True if name(...) is called anywhere inside node\"\"\"\n    return any(\n        isinstance(n, ast.Call) and isinstance(n.func, ast.Name) and n.func.id == name\n        for n in ast.walk(node)\n    )\n\n\ndef _extract_passwords(condition):\n    \"\"\"Pull string literals compared with != out of an 'and'-combined condition\"\"\"\n    passwords = []\n    comparisons = (\n        condition.values\n        if isinstance(condition, ast.BoolOp) and isinstance(condition.op, ast.And)\n        else [condition]\n    )\n    for comp in comparisons:\n        if not isinstance(comp, ast.Compare):\n            continue\n        if isinstance(comp.left, ast.Constant) and isinstance(comp.left.value, str):\n            passwords.append(comp.left.value)\n        for op, comparator in zip(comp.ops, comp.comparators):\n            if isinstance(op, ast.NotEq) and isinstance(comparator, ast.Constant) and isinstance(comparator.value, str):\n                passwords.append(comparator.value)\n    return passwords\n\n\nclass TestGuardTheGate(TestCase):\n    @classmethod\n    def setUpClass(cls):\n        \"\"\"Parse the source, then run the student's code once with mocked\n        input so we never hang waiting on real input(). If we can read real\n        passwords out of the while condition, we feed one wrong answer then\n        a valid one, so the same run checks both 'ask again' and 'let through.'\"\"\"\n        with open(\"main.py\") as f:\n            cls.source = f.read()\n        cls.tree = ast.parse(cls.source)\n        cls.while_nodes = [n for n in ast.walk(cls.tree) if isinstance(n, ast.While)]\n        cls.passwords = _extract_passwords(cls.while_nodes[0].test) if cls.while_nodes else []\n\n        if cls.passwords:\n            side_effect = [\"not-a-real-password\", cls.passwords[0]]\n        else:\n            side_effect = [\"not-a-real-password\"] * 5  # bounded, so a broken loop fails instead of hanging\n\n        cls.run_error = None\n        try:\n            with patch(\"builtins.input\", side_effect=side_effect) as mock_input:\n                cls.stdout = ValidationProtocol().get_stdout_logs()\n                cls.input_call_count = mock_input.call_count\n        except Exception:\n            cls.stdout = []\n            cls.input_call_count = len(side_effect)\n            cls.run_error = (\n                \"We couldn't get your program to accept a valid password within a few tries. \"\n                \"Check that your while condition uses 'and' (not 'or'), and that you're comparing \"\n                \"against the exact password text you chose.\"\n            )\n\n    def test_uses_while_loop_with_and_condition(self):\n        \"\"\"Uses a while loop whose condition combines checks with 'and'\"\"\"\n        self.assertTrue(\n            self.while_nodes,\n            \"We didn't find a while loop. Add a while loop whose condition checks that none of the passwords were given.\"\n        )\n        condition = self.while_nodes[0].test\n        self.assertTrue(\n            isinstance(condition, ast.BoolOp) and isinstance(condition.op, ast.And),\n            \"Your while condition should combine checks with 'and' (e.g., answer != 'open' and answer != 'sesame'), not 'or'. With 'or', the loop would stop almost immediately, since most answers make at least one comparison True.\"\n        )\n\n    def test_at_least_two_passwords(self):\n        \"\"\"Checks for at least 2 secret passwords in the while condition\"\"\"\n        if not self.while_nodes:\n            self.skipTest(\"Add your while loop first.\")\n        self.assertGreaterEqual(\n            len(set(self.passwords)),\n            2,\n            f\"We only found {len(set(self.passwords))} secret password(s) in your while condition. The task asks for at least 2.\"\n        )\n\n    def test_input_inside_loop_updates_condition_variable(self):\n        \"\"\"Stores the visitor's answer in a variable updated inside the loop\"\"\"\n        if not self.while_nodes:\n            self.skipTest(\"Add your while loop first.\")\n        loop = self.while_nodes[0]\n        condition_vars = {n.id for n in ast.walk(loop.test) if isinstance(n, ast.Name)}\n        body_nodes = []\n        for stmt in loop.body:\n            body_nodes.extend(ast.walk(stmt))\n        input_assigned_vars = {\n            t.id\n            for node in body_nodes\n            if isinstance(node, ast.Assign)\n            and isinstance(node.value, ast.Call)\n            and isinstance(node.value.func, ast.Name)\n            and node.value.func.id == \"input\"\n            for t in node.targets\n            if isinstance(t, ast.Name)\n        }\n        self.assertTrue(\n            input_assigned_vars,\n            \"We didn't find input() being called and stored in a variable inside your while loop. Make sure you're asking for a new answer each time through.\"\n        )\n        self.assertTrue(\n            condition_vars & input_assigned_vars,\n            \"The variable you update with input() inside the loop doesn't match the variable checked in your while condition. Make sure you're comparing the same variable you just asked for.\"\n        )\n\n    def test_asks_again_on_wrong_answer_then_accepts_valid_one(self):\n        \"\"\"Asks again on a wrong answer, then lets the visitor through on a valid password\"\"\"\n        if not self.while_nodes:\n            self.skipTest(\"Add your while loop first.\")\n        if not self.passwords:\n            self.skipTest(\"We need at least 2 real passwords in your while condition before we can test this.\")\n        if self.run_error:\n            self.fail(self.run_error)\n        self.assertEqual(\n            self.input_call_count,\n            2,\n            f\"Expected your program to ask for input exactly twice (once for the wrong answer, once for the valid password), but it asked {self.input_call_count} time(s).\"\n        )\n\n    def test_prints_confirmation_after_valid_password(self):\n        \"\"\"Prints a confirmation message once a valid password is given\"\"\"\n        if not self.while_nodes:\n            self.skipTest(\"Add your while loop first.\")\n        if not self.passwords:\n            self.skipTest(\"We need at least 2 real passwords in your while condition before we can test this.\")\n        if self.run_error:\n            self.fail(self.run_error)\n        # The starter code already prints 2 lines before input() is ever reached;\n        # anything beyond that must come from inside/after the loop.\n        self.assertGreater(\n            len(self.stdout),\n            2,\n            \"We didn't see a message printed after a valid password was entered. Make sure you print a confirmation once the loop ends.\"\n        )",
      "folderId": "0",
      "active": false,
      "type": "validation"
    },
    "long_instructions": "# Make: Guard the Gate\r\n\r\n**Your Task:** You’re building a riddle gate for a game. A guardian stands watch, and only lets a visitor through if they say one of the secret passwords. Anyone else gets turned away — and asked to try again.\r\n\r\n**New to this task:** using `input()` inside a loop, and comparing text answers with `!=`. If you get stuck on the syntax, ask **AI Tutor** to explain how `input()` or string comparison works.\r\n\r\nUse these rules:\r\n\r\n1. Choose 2 or more secret passwords that let a visitor through.\r\n2. Any other answer should make the guardian ask again.\r\n3. Once a valid password is given, print a message letting the visitor through.\r\n\r\nYour program should:\r\n\r\n- Use a `while` loop whose condition checks for “none of the passwords were given” (hint: this needs `and`, not `or`)\r\n- Store the visitor’s answer in a variable you update inside the loop\r\n- Print a confirmation message once a valid password is given\r\n\r\n\r\n**Do This:**\r\n\r\n1. Fill out MY PLAN in `guard_the_gate_plan.txt` before coding.\r\n2. Write your program in `main.py`, then use Validate to check it.\r\n3. Test your program with the input sequences in **MY TESTING**, including two sequences you choose.\r\n4. Trade seats with a partner. Try to trick your way past *their* guardian with a sneaky input. Record what you tried in **MY PARTNER TEST**. \r\n5. Fill out **MY REFLECTION**.\r\n\r\n**Challenge:**\r\n\r\nAfter Validate passes, add a second validated question the guardian asks before letting anyone through (e.g., a secret handshake word), or add a numeric rule using or (e.g., a visitor must guess a number in a valid range). Test your change with one input sequence in **MY TESTING.**"
  },
  "published": true,
